Put back what node's scopes count when their frames end without destructors.

A visorjs guest is built without exceptions. When the host engine runs out
of stack under node's C++ (entered from JavaScript at the stack limit), its
exception unwinds those frames and no destructor runs. An
InternalCallbackScope dropped that way leaves the callback scope depth and
the async id stack one entry up for good: the next callback scope to close
does not run the tick queue, and the process ends with "async hook stack
has become corrupted". A ShouldNotAbortOnUncaughtScope leaves its counter
up.

The shim marks its own counts at each entry the host makes into the guest
and restores them when an entry is unwound (<visorjs-unwind.h>). The
environment registers its three counts there: the callback scope depth, the
should-not-abort scopes, and the async id stack's length, which is popped
back as the dropped scopes' pop_async_context calls would have.

V8 has no such case: its API runs under headroom below the JavaScript
stack limit, so the overflow is thrown in JavaScript before the C++ runs.

--- a/src/env.cc
+++ b/src/env.cc
